# interplay_marathon_23: Marathons (pgtest/marathon_util.py): each seed is one scenario, interplay_marathon_<seed>, whose program the walker builds from the seed alone: one device session in which every subsystem is driven at once, step by step, so each subsystem's state changes while the others' work is in flight.  A step is one action of one subsystem, chosen at random among the legal ones: a display action (pgtest/randdisplay.py's walk: connects, acks, presents of backings and mapper surfaces with gamma tables and colour matrices, glyphs and cursors, sleep, ICC profiles, hot plugs and mode lists, scribbles, backings and surfaces rewritten, deleted, unmapped; with EFI at boot); a batch of GPU work (pgtest/dagwalk.py's order witnesses over the root FIFO, child FIFOs and one to three tasks, ordered by barriers, some nodes waiting on the gate, on earlier held work or on a faulted stamp); the gate opening; a fault on an idle child (pgtest/randfault.py's decode errors, an exec of a task that does not exist, unknown and deprecated opcodes) with work blocked on it, and its recovery by a resume or a delete, steps later; a lifetime action (pgtest/lifetime_util.py: the scratch buffer deleted, retyped in place or made again, pipelines and functions deleted, a task deleted and defined again) while work that names it is held; a memory change (pgtest/memlive_util.py: a scratch page moved by 34, a new leaf and 57; held work's command-buffer pages moved leaf by leaf or by a whole new leaf table, the old pages poisoned); child FIFOs deleted and defined again; pause (while paused, display host changes, commands rung, and the guest rewriting command pages and object-list entries), suspend onto a fresh device over held work, and reset or stop with the set-up again.  One model predicts every observation: the display walk's (callbacks, interrupts, 0x1014 and 0x1018, every shared page, the encoded pixels, present counts), kept exact across the GPU steps by counting their interrupts and stamp bits; every GPU slot against a control task's run of the same node, and the chain counters; every scratch page against the model of which RAM page the scratch object is bound to; the fault registers; and every wait succeeds but those labelled [held] (work shown not to run within 200 ms) and [dies]. python3 -m pgtest.marathon_util reads the expected files back against the model. Programs with mapper surfaces run in the map and isolate setups, the others in all three. Some programs end in a death docs/testing.md lists (7.3, 7.4) with work still held on the gate: a one-entry gamma table, the display doorbell or rvg_display_cursor_position while paused, a second pause, a guest mode count over 128.  This program: 2 ports, version 245, mapper surfaces; ack 1, connect 4, host_while_paused 2, move 1, plug 2, present_backing 3, rewrite_backing 1, ring_while_paused 3, scribble 1, set_modes 1, unpause 2, unplug 2; ends in a death (mode_count_over). GPU: 2 children, 2 tasks (depths [1, 1]), gate p 0.25; steps: churn 1, cmd_move 3, dag 11, display 23, fault 2, life 3, pause 2, recover 2, release 2, scr_move 2, suspend 5, teardown 4; nodes: dag 28, fault opcode 1, fault overlong 1.
